# The user database, in the format Darwin reads it.
#
# THIS FILE IS NOT INTERCHANGEABLE WITH /etc/passwd. Libinfo's file module picks its source by
# effective uid: at euid 0 it opens /etc/master.passwd and NOTHING ELSE, and returns NULL if that
# open fails. A guest here runs as root, so with no master.passwd every getpwuid answered NULL no
# matter what /etc/passwd contained.
#
# The cost of that was three layers away from anything about users: LibreOffice resolves
# UserInstallation=$SYSUSERCONFIG/LibreOffice/4 through osl_getConfigDir, which is built on the
# passwd entry rather than on $HOME, so with no entry there was no configuration directory and
# bootstrap failed as "Unspecified Application Error".
#
# Ten colon separated fields, which is what distinguishes this format from /etc/passwd's seven:
#   name:password:uid:gid:class:change:expire:gecos:home:shell
#
# The home directory matches the container's HOME rather than the /var/root a real Mac uses,
# because that is where this prefix actually puts it and a passwd entry that disagrees with the
# filesystem is worse than none.
root:*:0:0::0:0:System Administrator:/Users/root:/bin/sh
nobody:*:-2:-2::0:0:Unprivileged User:/var/empty:/usr/bin/false
#
# SERVICE ACCOUNTS, because a launchd job that names a UserName it cannot resolve DOES NOT RUN.
# com.apple.trustd asks for _trustd, and with only root and nobody here the job exited 3 every time
# it was demand-started -- silently, since its plist ships no StandardErrorPath. Nothing above that
# said "unknown user": it surfaced six layers up as a code signature being refused, because with no
# trustd there is no trust evaluation at all.
#
# The ids follow macOS's service-account range. What actually matters here is only that the account
# EXISTS and is unprivileged; if a parity check ever finds macOS uses different numbers, this is a
# one-line change.
_trustd:*:282:282::0:0:Trust Services:/var/empty:/usr/bin/false
_lp:*:26:26::0:0:Printing Services:/var/spool/cups:/usr/bin/false
